   ],
   "size": 6.6
  },
  {
   "block_id": "p002b13",
   "page": 2,
   "source": "pages/p0002.txt",
   "char_start": 816,
   "char_end": 825,
   "text": "4 Results",
   "bbox": [
    307.5,
    69.4,
    355.5,
    80.2
   ],
   "size": 10.8
  },
  {
   "block_id": "p002b14",
   "page": 2,
   "source": "pages/p0002.txt",
   "char_start": 827,
   "char_end": 1118,
   "text": "Table 1 reports the main comparison. The baseline recovers 71.4% of body characters in the correct position, leaks 2 running-head lines per page, and its block order correlates with the ground truth at tau = 0.41. The column-aware extractor reaches 98.1%, leaks none, and reaches tau = 0.97.",
   "bbox": [
    307.5,
    86.3,
    520.7,
    150.9
   ],
   "size": 8.6
  },
  {
   "block_id": "p002b15",
   "page": 2,
   "source": "pages/p0002.txt",
   "char_start": 1120,
   "char_end": 1331,
   "text": "The residual 1.9% is concentrated in the table: cells whose text wraps across two lines are merged, because the current implementation joins lines by vertical proximity before it splits cells by horizontal gaps.",
   "bbox": [
    307.5,
    156.5,
    521.1,
    198.7
   ],
   "size": 8.6
  },
  {
   "block_id": "p002b16",
   "page": 2,
   "source": "pages/p0002.txt",
   "char_start": 1333,
   "char_end": 1461,
   "text": "Equation numbers are recovered exactly in both conditions because they sit at the right margin and survive content-stream order.",
   "bbox": [
    307.5,
    201.3,
    499.2,
    232.3
   ],
   "size": 8.6
  },
  {
   "block_id": "p003b01",
   "page": 3,
   "source": "pages/p0003.txt",
   "char_start": 0,
   "char_end": 12,
   "text": "5 Discussion",
   "bbox": [
    52.0,
    69.4,
    116.2,
    80.2
   ],
   "size": 10.8
  },
  {
   "block_id": "p003b02",
   "page": 3,
   "source": "pages/p0003.txt",
   "char_start": 14,
   "char_end": 171,
   "text": "Reading order is a precondition for every downstream task, and it is cheap to verify: a character range either re-slices to the recorded text or it does not.",
   "bbox": [
    52.0,
    86.3,
    256.6,
    117.3
   ],
   "size": 8.6
  },
  {
   "block_id": "p003b03",
   "page": 3,
   "source": "pages/p0003.txt",
   "char_start": 173,
   "char_end": 322,
   "text": "We therefore report ranges rather than prose confidence, and we mark a block unresolved instead of guessing when its glyphs carry no usable position.",
   "bbox": [
    52.0,
    119.9,
    261.4,
    150.9
   ],
   "size": 8.6
  },
  {
   "block_id": "p003b04",
   "page": 3,
   "source": "pages/p0003.txt",
   "char_start": 324,
   "char_end": 569,
   "text": "Two limits deserve mention. Pages whose columns are separated by a rule rather than whitespace defeat the cut test, and scanned pages have no glyph positions at all until OCR runs. Both are reported as unresolved blocks with the reason attached.",
   "bbox": [
    52.0,
    156.5,
    261.8,
    209.9
   ],
   "size": 8.6
  },
  {
   "block_id": "p003b05",
   "page": 3,
   "source": "pages/p0003.txt",
   "char_start": 571,
   "char_end": 583,
   "text": "6 Conclusion",
   "bbox": [
    52.0,
    213.8,
    117.4,
    224.6
   ],
   "size": 10.8
  },
  {
   "block_id": "p003b06",
   "page": 3,
   "source": "pages/p0003.txt",
   "char_start": 585,
   "char_end": 905,
   "text": "A whitespace-cut ordering pass, a frequency filter for running heads, and per-block character ranges together raise correct-position recall from 71.4% to 98.1% on a controlled two-column benchmark. The gain is measurable, the failure modes are named, and every reported block can be re-sliced from the emitted page text.",
   "bbox": [
    52.0,
    230.6,
    264.2,
    306.4
   ],
   "size": 8.6
  },
  {
   "block_id": "p003b07",
   "page": 3,
   "source": "pages/p0003.txt",
   "char_start": 907,
   "char_end": 917,
   "text": "References",
   "bbox": [
    307.5,
    69.4,
    362.7,
    80.2
   ],
   "size": 10.8
  },
  {
